# Python
.venv/
# The separate environment for the text-to-speech narration tooling.
.venv-tts/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/

# Node / web
web/node_modules/
web/dist/
web/test-results/
web/playwright-report/

# Local secrets and machine-specific config
.env

# Rendered from infra/kratos/kratos.yml.template by
# scripts/render_ports_env.py, the same way .env's ports get rendered --
# a generated file, edit config.json and re-render instead of this one.
infra/kratos/kratos.yml

# The quickstart worker's own scratch space, if MUNITAS_HOST_WORK_DIR
# points inside the checkout. Machine-local, same reasoning as verify/history/.
quickstart-work/

# This machine's own record of its verification runs, written by
# run-verification.ps1. The tooling is source; the runs are not.
verify/history/

# OS
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Editor
.vscode/
.idea/

# Per-line intermediate audio from synthesising the consultation recordings.
# The committed recording is the finished <topic>-synthetic.wav beside them,
# which scripts/seed/seed-health-example.py uploads; these are regenerated by
# each folder's synthesize.py.
docs/audio-samples/*/shots/

# Screenshots captured for the docs/ walkthroughs. The committed artefact is
# the HTML page, which carries every image inline; these PNGs are scratch and
# are rewritten whenever the capture runs.
web/walkthroughs/shots/

# The Python environment may be a link to another drive rather than a folder,
# and a pattern ending in / matches only real folders.
.venv

# Local working notes for the AI assistant used on this project: kept beside
# the code on this machine, not published.
CLAUDE.md
.claude/

# Local session status note; never published.
SESSION_STATUS.md

# Everything under docs/internal/: architecture notes, hand-authored
# diagrams, engineering-status tracking, design rationale documents that
# name a real gap's before/after severity, and other content written for
# deep context (a contributor or an AI assistant) rather than for a
# customer or public reader. docs/public/ is the published side; the split
# is physical so which is which never depends on remembering a gitignore
# entry per file. See docs/public/design/governance-model.md for what a
# public reader gets instead.
docs/internal/

# One-off investigations (e.g. model comparison trials): useful locally,
# not something to publish or keep in sync across machines.
investigations/

# The local TLS certificate and private key infra/tls/generate-local-cert.sh
# generates for the API's HTTPS listener (worker <-> API only, see
# RUNBOOK.md's "Local HTTPS between the worker and the API"). A private key
# never belongs in git; regenerating this is one command, not something
# worth carrying across machines.
infra/tls/out/

# Deliberately kept on disk, deliberately never committed: overrides this
# platform's own immutability guarantee for a named, explicit list of
# already-sealed datasets, on a tenant that may be production. Real and
# reusable (unlike a scratchpad one-off), which is exactly why it stays
# local rather than shipping in the repo history everyone who clones this
# gets -- a tool this sharp is safer kept, and kept quiet, than deleted and
# rewritten from memory under pressure next time it's actually needed.
scripts/admin/purge-dataset-versions.py

# docs/internal/dev/services.html and logins.html (generated by
# docs/tools/generate_services_page.py, run automatically by
# start-dev.ps1) are covered by the docs/internal/ rule above. logins.html
# names every seeded person's real login email and the one shared dev
# password in plain text; services.html names every internal service URL
# and port this machine is actually running. Regenerate either locally
# any time with that same script.
